WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:06.630
HENRY BRINK: Well, good evening.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.100
I am Henry Brink.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.200
I'm a senior at
Hillsdale College.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.160
I'm an English major.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.200
And I'm most recently
from St. Paul, Minnesota.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.710
And it is my pleasure to
introduce Mr. Nathaniel

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.680
Philbrick this evening.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.440
Nathaniel Philbrick is an
author and a research fellow

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.990
at the Nantucket
Historical Association.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.780
He earned his BA from
Brown University and his MA

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.490
from Duke University.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.409
Mr. Philbrick is the
recipient of numerous awards,

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.280
including the Samuel Eliot
Morison Award from the USS

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.820
Constitution Museum, the William
Bradford award from Pilgrim

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.010
Society, and a
Lifetime Achievement

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.700
Award from the New England
Historic Genealogical Society.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.410
He's the author
of numerous books,

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.660
including In the Heart of the
Sea, which won a National Book

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.780
Award for nonfiction and
was the basis of the 2015

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.230
movie of the same name, The
Last Stand, Custer, Sitting

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.360
Bull, and the Battle
of Little Bighorn,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.450
and most recently, In
the Hurricane's Eye,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.020
the Genius of George Washington
and the Victory at Yorktown.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.930
So would you please help me
in welcoming Mr. Nathaniel

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.342
Philbrick?

00:01:11.342 --> 00:01:14.318
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:20.338 --> 00:01:21.880
NATHANIEL PHILBRICK:
Well, thank you.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.310
It is a great
delight to be here.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.580
Last time I was here, I was
speaking about George Armstrong

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:28.540
Custer.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.690
So we're now going to
change venues a little bit

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.000
and go with George Washington.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.630
And I have to say, it was
after finishing The Last Stand,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:41.440
my book about Custer, that
I was searching for topics.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.570
And you know, most
of my books tend

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.340
to focus on
communities of people.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.110
Whether it's the 20 crew
members of the whale ship Essex

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.970
that have just been
rammed by a whale

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.220
and thrown into a
survival situation,

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.550
or 102 pilgrims about
to find themselves

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:02.230
on a coast about which they know
nothing in the fall of 1620,

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:06.340
or Custer's regiment, bound
for the Little Bighorn,

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.230
where there is a Lakota
village of unimaginable size,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.480
I'm interested in what
happens to communities

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.370
under terrific stress.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.070
And I live on Nantucket Island.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.750
It's a community with
a year-round population

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.020
of about 15,000.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.710
And our daughter, when she was--

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.080
I was a stay-at-home
dad growing up.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.980
And we lived briefly in historic
Boston, the historic core,

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.690
which is the North End.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.970
And I remember
pushing the stroller

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.890
through the North End
of Boston, imagining

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.520
what Boston was like in
the time of the Revolution.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.970
And Boston, now, looks
entirely different from what

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:49.690
it did then.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.080
Back then, it was a
1.1 square mile island,

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:58.870
dominated by three hills
of almost mountainous size,

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.930
with a population
of 15,000 connected

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.930
by a thin neck of land to
the mainland at Roxbury.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:09.550
And I began to think, after
moving to Nantucket, 15,000--

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:13.190
I know what it's like to live
in a community of 15,000.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.450
You go to the grocery store.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.550
You don't know everyone by
name, but you recognize them.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.730
It's a personal.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:22.012
When you get to a stop sign
and someone blows you off,

00:03:22.012 --> 00:03:23.470
you don't say
anything because that

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:27.995
could be your kid's teacher or
your dental hygienist, which

00:03:27.995 --> 00:03:29.620
is really-- you don't
want to-- anyway,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.600
so it's a personal way
of looking at things.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.190
And so what would
have been like to be

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.760
in Boston as the pressures
of a revolution mounted?

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.810
And so that led to my
writing Bunker Hill.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:48.160
And that battle is the
obvious sort of plot point

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.490
that everything leads to.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:53.410
But I found myself
unexpectedly taken

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.900
with a gentleman who arrives
just a few weeks later,

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.390
George Washington.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.130
Now, I had grown up with George
Washington, like all of us

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.820
do, with George staring
at me from the $1 bill.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.843
And you know, this is the
guy of a statue, you know?

00:04:06.843 --> 00:04:09.550
I, at that point, thought,
what could be more

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.430
boring than George Washington?

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.740
But the George Washington
I was introduced

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:22.150
to in Boston in the summer of
1775 was anything but boring

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.000
and very different from
what he would become--

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.000
the defensive-minded
general that

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.070
would hold a nation together.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.180
He was wild-eyed.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.880
He was a lot like George Custer.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.690
This was George in his
early 40s, red-haired.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.610
He didn't like
the New Englanders

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.370
he had inherited in
the provincial army.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.290
You know, New England
has a tradition.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:47.790
We have it on Nantucket, the
tradition of the town meeting.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.890
And this is democracy
at its bare-knuckled

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:53.230
best, where, you
have a town meeting,

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.500
and you basically fight
it out on the town floor,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.220
and somehow, you
come to a decision.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.170
So these were people--

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:00.910
these were young
men who had grown up

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.950
in these kinds of
situations where they always

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:06.440
had a say in what was
happening to them.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.890
And so when Washington
would deliver them an order,

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:12.070
they would say,
whoa, wait a minute.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.290
If we decide we
want to do this, we

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:16.990
will be very happy to
fulfill your order.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.170
And this just drove
Washington crazy.

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:22.000
He was used to a
hierarchical society.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.350
And so Washington saw
this group of militiamen

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.090
and just wanted to
get it over with.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.050
Here, the British
were in Boston.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:35.120
They were besieged by
the provincial army.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.270
And this was a group of
professional soldiers.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:43.630
He had these militiamen, no
money, not enough gunpowder.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:46.780
There was no way he could
see fighting a long war.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.280
He wanted to get it over with.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.760
He wanted to attack
Boston, take it by storm,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.350
burn it down, if necessary, and
just get this thing over with.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:06:01.420
Three times he would bring that
proposal to his council of war.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.470
Three times it would be
voted down unanimously.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.890
And ultimately, they would go
with a much more conservative

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:15.730
plan of bringing his young,
soon-to-be head of artillery,

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.510
Henry Knox, the bookseller who
had learned everything he knew

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.630
about military
through those books--

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:26.220
God bless, booksellers-- and
would bring down the cannons

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.290
from Fort Ticonderoga.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.500
A few of them would then end
up on Dorchester Heights,

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.220
giving them a commanding
view over Boston.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.300
And General Howe, who was
then the head of the--

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.130
leading the British
army, realized,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.240
we got to get out of here.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.210
And thus would come the
evacuation of Boston

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.700
in March of that year.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.370
And I just had to follow George.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:55.260
What was going to-- how would
he become the Washington

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.660
who would be capable of
holding the country together

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.950
through eight years of war and
establishing the presidency?

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:02.540
How would he--

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:05.310
So that led me to my next
book about the Revolution,

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.430
Valiant Ambition, in
which I paired Washington

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.710
with Benedict Arnold.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.700
We have people of
different arcs.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:19.170
The book begins in
the summer of 1776.

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:22.650
By now, the British
have left Boston,

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:25.920
but the empire is
about to strike back.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.410
And it's New York which
is in their sights.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:34.440
And a huge fleet arrives
in New York, of ultimately,

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.890
40,000 soldiers and sailors,
and a fleet of 400 vessels.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.730
This is more people than
in all of Philadelphia,

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:45.830
the largest urban center in
North America at that time.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:47.520
And there is
Washington, dug into New

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.200
York and the high
ground in Brooklyn.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.140
He's completely
out-generalled in what

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.200
will be called the
Battle of Long Island.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.780
But what he is brilliant
at is retreats,

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.080
which he'll be doing a
lot of in the years ahead.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.730
The nighttime retreat
across the East River

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:10.020
is one of the more amazing
logistical feats in which he

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.830
delivers his army to
the island of Manhattan,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.110
under the noses of the
British, who are completely

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.090
unaware that this is happening.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:21.330
He then is forced to
retreat all the way to--

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.390
ultimately, across New
Jersey to the Pennsylvania

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.490
side of the Delaware.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:27.840
And it's really bad.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.660
He's lost most of his
army to desertion.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.890
And then will come
the famous Battle

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:39.390
of Trenton, in which he turns
it round on Christmas Day, which

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:41.409
is an incredible feat.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:44.520
And it's Washington at his best.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.900
It's Washington at the lowest.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:50.430
But what Washington
had an ability to do

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.360
is Washington had a--

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.820
and I speak of him--
he had a true genius.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.670
He wasn't a genius
like, let's say,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.880
Founding Fathers Thomas
Jefferson and Hamilton.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:05.280
He was not a jittery, brilliant
person, eloquent, and a mind

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.750
that could go anywhere.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:10.950
He had the ability to dial
out the static of life.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:14.550
And believe me, as Washington
retreated across New Jersey

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.950
to the other side of
the Delaware River,

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.170
there was a lot of
static out there,

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:22.230
a lot of people, a
lot of naysayers.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:25.110
He had the ability
to say, OK, what

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:29.910
is the most important
thing for me to do now?

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:32.190
And so there on the
Delaware, he realized,

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.512
we've got to have some kind
of comeback strategy here.

00:09:35.512 --> 00:09:37.470
If we're going to do it,
we have to shock them.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.440
It's the only way we
can turn this around.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:43.110
He succeeds in doing that.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:46.950
It's one of the most amazing
comebacks in military history.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.820
But what I began to
realize with Washington,

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:55.230
his genius was also, and
primarily so, political.

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:59.550
He was very good at
working with others,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.810
listening to different
sides, subtly moving people

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.820
in directions that,
from his point of view,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.300
were the right way to go.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.200
And when it came to
fighting this revolution,

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.060
he had to deal with Congress.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.240
This was-- you know, we
were creating a republic.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.100
And the great
danger when it came

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.440
to revolutions and
republics that all of them

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.560
would end with a military coup.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.040
The civil government in
the wake of a revolution

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.290
is inefficient, frustrating.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.720
The military,
whether it's Caesar,

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.180
whether it's
Cromwell in England,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.050
or in the future, Napoleon,
someone takes control,

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.450
and the dream ends.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.360
And this is what the Continental
Congress was fearful of.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:45.000
So they kept Washington
on a very tight leash.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.200
And one of the paradoxes is that
the more successful Washington

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:54.420
got, the more
untrustworthy congressmen

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.150
became, particularly those
in the South, where the--

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:00.833
excuse me, those in New England,
where the Revolution had begun.

00:11:00.833 --> 00:11:02.250
And then the
following year, would

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:06.780
come the Battle of
Saratoga, in which,

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:11.070
largely, through the
heroics of Benedict Arnold,

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.520
America would score a great
victory, with Horatio Gates

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.170
as the commanding officer.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.780
Meanwhile, Washington
lost a series

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:22.350
of battles, the Battle of
Brandywine and Germantown,

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:28.440
allowing the British William
Howe to move into Philadelphia.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:32.100
This got the politicians to
wondering whether Washington

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:33.750
was the right person.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:39.540
His army is dug in to a Valley
Forge, that terrible winter.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:44.010
And there is an
attempt to replace

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.990
Washington with Horatio Gates.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:50.520
It's unsuccessful, largely
because of Washington's

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.350
political brilliance.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.480
He was one of these people,
he was not necessarily

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.570
the greatest military
strategist in the world,

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:59.880
but he was terrific at
working with people,

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:03.630
at seeing the big
side of things.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:08.790
And his integrity was
never, never doubted.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:12.390
He succeeds in getting through
that terrible winter at Valley

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:13.140
Forge.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:17.040
And from then on, particularly
with a stellar performance

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.940
at the Battle of
Monmouth, he's in.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.350
He is the unquestioned face of
not only the Continental Army,

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.830
but he is becoming
the face of America,

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:31.740
and absolutely
essential to all this.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.230
One of his best generals,
Benedict Arnold,

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:36.120
moves in the other direction.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:42.030
He is denied a promotion by
the Continental Congress,

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:45.960
which were the ones
doling out the promotions

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.640
to major general.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:49.670
He ultimately decides that it--

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.710
while Washington's destiny
to hold the country together,

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:56.170
it's his destiny to try to
tear that country apart.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:13:00.420
And he unsuccessfully attempts
to surrender West Point, where

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.070
there are 3,000 soldiers
and all sorts of armaments

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:05.880
and ammunition.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.760
He unsuccessfully-- he
attempts to surrender that

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:09.750
to the British.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:15.460
That happens in the
late summer of 1780.

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:20.530
That is foiled, but America
is at an absolute low point.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.780
You know, we Americans
went into this revolution

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:26.800
because we did not want to
pay taxes to Great Britain.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:29.650
We apparently did not want
to pay the taxes required

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.510
to support Washington's army.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.730
It was withering on the vine.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:37.810
Recruitment levels in the
states were miserable.

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:41.000
There just was nothing going on.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:42.820
But there was good news.

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:47.140
In 1778, after that great
victory at Saratoga,

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:51.310
France had decided to enter
the revolution on our side.

00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:53.050
And this is terrific
because France

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:58.510
had what the American army did
not have, an effective navy.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:01.300
Up until this point,
Great Britain's navy,

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:04.150
the most powerful in the
world, had a stranglehold

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:06.160
on the Eastern Seaboard.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.250
They could move
troops at will by sea,

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:12.280
with relatively
lightning speed, enabling

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.950
them to take Charleston,
to move up and down.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:17.470
Washington knew the
only way that they

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:21.100
had a shot at a victory
that would potentially

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:23.110
end this thing in
a favorable way

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:26.500
was with the help
of the French navy.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.200
Now, previously, France
had been humiliated

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:31.780
by the British in
the Seven Years

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:34.480
War, or the French
and Indian War.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:35.920
They had lost Canada.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:41.860
Their ally, Spain, had
lost Florida, New Orleans.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:43.990
But after that,
France had launched

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:48.280
into a program that they
called [FRENCH],, revenge.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.840
And they realized,
how are we going

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:54.190
to regain the status we had
before the Seven Years War?

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:56.920
And that was by
retooling their navy.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:01.510
They created a naval academy
at Brest, which still exists.

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:03.670
A professor there was
hugely helpful to me

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:06.880
in writing my book, In
the Hurricane's Eye.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.050
They analyzed naval
warfare analytically,

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:18.220
applying the ideals of that time
to analyze what was going on,

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:21.190
and came up with tactical guides
that were way beyond anything

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:22.270
the British had.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:25.160
In fact, the British
were using those guys

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:28.690
to help develop
their own tactics.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:32.330
They also were retooling their
navy, building better ships

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.340
than the British had.

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.550
By this time, it had been
developed that the best

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:37.923
ship of the line--

00:15:37.923 --> 00:15:40.090
and a ship of the-- it was
called a ship of the line

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:43.130
because the way two fleets
would attack each other

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:45.550
was they'd create
a line of battle,

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.550
and the two fleets would come
to within firing distance,

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:51.580
sometimes only a
pistol shot away,

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:54.730
and blast away at each
other until one side began

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:56.590
to give way.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:59.950
And it was realized that a 74--

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:02.530
this is a ship with 74 guns--

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:06.130
was the optimal size
for a ship of the line.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:07.170
And these were--

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:08.670
You know, I think
we have a tendency

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:11.800
to think of square-rigged
ships as kind

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:15.880
of quaint forms of
transportation from the past.

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:18.910
But a 74 was the
most sophisticated

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.730
technological creation
of the 18th century.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:25.210
Between 500 and 750 men aboard.

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:28.030
It took 2,000 oak
trees to build one.

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:31.630
That's 73 acres of forest.

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:36.700
If one of these sailed into a
harbor on the American coast,

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.440
they would take
over because they

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:42.670
had as many cannons
as Washington's army

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:44.530
at the beginning of the army.

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:49.000
And so Washington
needed the French navy.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.060
And this is where
his genius comes in.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:54.910
As soon as France entered,
he realized what he needed,

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.310
more than anything
else, was for France

00:16:57.310 --> 00:17:01.060
to establish naval
superiority somewhere

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:04.540
on the Atlantic coast, where he
could coordinate with his army

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:07.900
and win that victory
that would end the war.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:11.290
But he quickly realized this
was going to be difficult.

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:13.780
Because what had happened
with the entry of France

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:17.200
and then their ally, Spain,
was that a colonial rebellion

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.150
became a world war.

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:22.540
Now, you had England
fighting France and Spain.

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:27.550
The war moved as far as India.

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:31.030
But the real center of wealth
in the world at this time

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:35.370
was the Caribbean, the sugar
islands of the West Indies,

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:40.360
Jamaica, French Haiti.

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:42.820
For the Spaniards, it was Cuba.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.000
Most of their income was
coming from these islands.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:49.980
And so once France and
Spain entered the war,

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:53.350
the ships come in, but they
all go down to the Caribbean

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:56.780
and fight it out, trading
islands back and forth,

00:17:56.780 --> 00:17:58.970
much to the frustration
of Washington, who

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:03.640
stuck outside New York, where
now, General Sir Henry Clinton

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:06.430
is dug in with 10,000 soldiers.

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:09.070
Washington is on the Hudson,
sort of glaring at him,

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:10.600
for two years.

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.570
The war has basically
in the Northeast

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.560
has turned into a stalemate.

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.560
Washington complains
in a letter,

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:22.940
"this war drags on like
a cart with no wheels."

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:25.780
What is positively
happening is happen--

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:27.290
what is happening
is in the South.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:30.570
And it's not good for America.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.080
The British take Charleston.

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:37.400
And then Lord Cornwallis
meets the hero

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:41.870
of Saratoga, Horatio Gates,
at Camden, South Carolina.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:44.750
And Horatio Gates is
completely annihilated.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:47.330
He literally runs
from the battlefield.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.480
And the American
army is scattered.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:54.980
Washington decides to send the
general who has become really

00:18:54.980 --> 00:19:00.860
his most trustworthy
general, Nathanael Greene,

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:03.080
raised a Quaker in Rhode Island.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:06.350
On Nantucket, we love our
Quakers with a vengeance.

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:09.960
And Greene would
make his way south.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.490
So that was happening.

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:13.950
But what Washington
desperately wanted

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:17.670
was finally to get something
happening with the navy.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:22.230
And then, in the fall of
1780, this is almost a year

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:25.470
precisely before it would
be the victory at Yorktown,

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.740
something happens in the
Caribbean-- three hurricanes

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.490
in the span of a month.

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:35.820
The first hurricane would
destroy much of Cuba,

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:40.170
and Jamaica, and a good
portion of the fleets

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:44.290
of both of France and
England, and along with Spain.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.110
But it would be--
the second hurricane

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:50.370
is now known as the
Great Storm of 1780.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:55.740
Winds that have been estimated
had to be 200 miles an hour.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:20:00.030
Because what happened was
the trees on these islands

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:04.110
were power-washed of their
bark, you know, just stripped,

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.630
every structure annihilated.

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:12.330
22,000 people would die in the
Caribbean in that one storm.

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:17.100
And then, just a few weeks
later, as the Spanish navy

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:21.720
has just departed Cuba
with 5,000 soldiers,

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:27.320
a fleet of 40 ships, what's
known as Solano's Hurricane

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:27.820
hits.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:30.550
That's the admiral in
charge of the Spanish fleet.

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:32.310
Wipes out that fleet.

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:35.170
And the wreckage is forced to
flee back to Cuba before they

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:39.520
can attack
British-held Pensacola,

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:41.990
because that's
Spain's real goal.

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:44.830
And then this is the
message that gets delivered

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:47.000
to France for the first time.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:49.810
They realize, next
summer and fall,

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:52.780
we don't want to leave our
fleet in the Caribbean.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:54.160
We've got to move them north.

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:57.670
We have to do as Washington's
requesting and come north.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:00.940
This is the beginning
of the year of Yorktown,

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:04.240
and it starts with a hurricane.

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:07.270
Next thing that happens
is Benedict Arnold,

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:10.480
Britain's newest
brigadier general,

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:14.500
is sent by Clinton
to the Chesapeake.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.810
Benedict Arnold quickly
proves he might be

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:19.370
the best general on both sides.

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:21.910
He speeds up the
James River, soon

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:24.970
has the wartime
capital of Richmond,

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:30.010
which had been moved that far
inland under the assumption

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:34.620
that it would be too far
away for an enemy to attack.

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:37.020
Benedict Arnold strikes
with lightning speed.

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:40.620
Governor Thomas Jefferson
is forced to flee.

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.570
It's in flames.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:45.750
This obviously
angers Washington,

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:48.420
who sends down the young
French general, who's

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:50.940
become a kind of
surrogate son, Lafayette,

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:55.020
to do battle as best he
can with Benedict Arnold.

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:57.390
In the meantime,
Nathanael Greene

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:02.160
has been doing everything he
can to consolidate his army.

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:05.400
And he and Lord
Cornwallis have it

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:08.640
out after what's known
as the Race to the Dan,

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:12.010
across the breadth
of North Carolina.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:13.620
Well, at that point,
Greene doesn't

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:16.410
have the soldiers to
face the 2,000 crack

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:18.270
troops under Lord Cornwallis.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.430
He gets over the
Virginia border,

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:24.860
is able to swell his army with
militiaman, dives back south.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:30.810
And they meet in the Battle
of Guilford Courthouse.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:33.960
Greene does not win
because Lord Cornwallis

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:35.250
has the field at the end.

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:38.640
But he has eviscerated
Cornwallis's army,

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:42.930
that's lost half their
best officers and men.

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:47.790
Cornwallis retreats to
Wilmington to refit, to retool.

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:52.110
But being someone
who is aggressive,

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.630
he decides, rather than
retreat to Charleston

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:58.140
and consolidate the
forces in South Carolina,

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:03.210
to move into Virginia, where
he replaces Benedict Arnold.

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:06.780
And he and Lafayette
battle it out

00:23:06.780 --> 00:23:09.730
in a fascinating
cat-and-mouse game.

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:14.730
Meanwhile, Washington is in
talks with French General

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:21.390
Rochambeau, who is stationed
in Newport with 5,000 soldiers.

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:25.860
Washington is desperate for news
about where is the French navy.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:28.260
Are you going to help me?

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:31.380
Unfortunately, Rochambeau
has been instructed by France

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:35.760
not to inform Washington of the
whereabouts of the French navy.

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.950
Their fear is that
the American war

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.930
effort is about to collapse.

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:43.560
They realize that recruitment
levels are not happening.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:46.580
It looks like it's all
just going to fall apart.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:50.880
And in case that
happens, the fleet

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:55.020
that leaves Brest that winter
under Admiral de Grasse

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:58.500
has been instructed to
then sail to Newport,

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:03.210
evacuate Rochambeau's army,
and abandon North America.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:05.370
Can you imagine
if that happened?

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:09.840
So they don't want Washington to
know quite where de Grasse is.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:12.660
Washington and Rochambeau have
a meeting and Wethersfield,

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:15.060
Connecticut to discuss strategy.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:16.770
Washington feels
what they should

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:21.450
do is attack the British
dug into New York.

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:24.120
It's the only place where there
are enough soldiers, where

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:28.270
there could be a victory
big enough to end the war.

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:32.310
The French, however, are fearful
of entering New York Harbor.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:34.090
They like the Chesapeake.

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:37.950
Rochambeau tells
George Washington, OK,

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.770
we'll do what you say-- because
Washington is the nominal head

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:42.390
of the allied force--

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:46.200
but secretly informs Admiral
de Grasse, who, by this time,

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:49.050
has left Brest and
is in the Caribbean,

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:53.640
that when you come north, you
should go to the Chesapeake.

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.740
It's not until June
that Washington

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:59.370
reads in the newspaper
of de Grasse's activities

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:01.700
in the West Indies.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:02.980
He confronts Rochambeau.

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:04.090
So what's going on here?

00:25:04.090 --> 00:25:06.510
Oh, yes, we've been talking.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:07.120
Don't worry.

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.330
Things will work out.

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:14.130
It's August 14 when Washington--
by this time, Rochambeau's army

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:18.180
has moved from
Newport to New York,

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:21.060
combined with Washington's
army on the Hudson.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:24.810
Washington gets a letter
from de Grasse informing him

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:28.890
that de Grasse is sailing not
for New York, as he thought

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:31.740
was agreed upon, but
for the Chesapeake.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:34.680
There's two
congressional delegates

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:38.970
who are there with Washington
when he gets this letter.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:42.550
Apparently, he was very angry.

00:25:42.550 --> 00:25:44.280
They go to prepare
for breakfast.

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:46.180
They meet up with
him for breakfast.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:47.610
And he's completely calmed down.

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:49.080
And this is Washington.

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:56.610
Today, we try to fulfill our
destinies as a personality.

00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:58.230
You know, what is
our inner core?

00:25:58.230 --> 00:25:59.490
Let's become that.

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:01.470
Washington knew his inner core.

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:04.350
His inner core was that of
someone with anger management

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:06.720
issues, and he
spent his whole life

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:10.080
trying to control
that anger management.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:14.550
He very famously studied
the rules of civility.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:19.680
He was under incredible
amounts of self-control.

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.780
But every now and then, he
just couldn't take it anymore.

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:26.220
He would vent it, but
then always subsume

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:30.460
those emotions to do the
best thing for his country.

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:33.720
He calms down, and he says to
the congressional delegates,

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:36.310
well, the plan has changed.

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:39.120
We are now going
to march from New

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:45.510
York 500 miles to Williamsburg.

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:48.450
And his New
Englanders do not want

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:52.200
to march in August into the
killing heat of the South.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.000
He says, we're
going to need help.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.840
Do your best to help us.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.680
And then this is
Washington at his best.

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:01.620
He's in a situation he
didn't want to be in,

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:04.500
but he's going to do the
best to make it work.

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:06.870
And this is where he
enlisted the same skills

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:10.830
he showed during that retreat
across the East River.

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:15.930
He does an amazing job of
fooling General Clinton in New

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:21.840
York, convincing him that
the allied forces are

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:25.770
going to attack New
York via Staten Island.

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:28.380
Letters are leaked,
are supposedly

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:33.390
stolen that Clinton gets
that reinforce all this.

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:36.510
Washington's not even
telling Rochambeau everything

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:41.280
he's thinking of doing because
he knows secrecy is paramount.

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:43.350
They begin to
march the soldiers.

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:45.270
And by this time--
one of the reasons

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:47.550
Washington did
not want to attack

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:50.130
the Chesapeake was that
Lord Cornwallis was

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:53.310
moving around Virginia.

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:55.750
He wasn't staying
in any one place.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:58.350
And if the large allied
force moved down there,

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:01.470
he would just move into
South Carolina and escape.

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.390
And in August,
that's what they all

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:07.518
assumed Cornwallis would
do-- once it became obvious

00:28:07.518 --> 00:28:09.060
that they were headed
down, he'd just

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:12.270
move into North Carolina and
eventually South Carolina.

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:14.760
And there would--
he would escape.

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:18.660
But for reasons that are
still not clear, by this time,

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:23.130
Cornwallis is dug into a town
at the end of the point formed

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:24.960
by the James and York Rivers--

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:25.860
Yorktown.

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:30.570
He's digging in to form
a potential naval base.

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:32.790
Suddenly, he's
got-- there he is.

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:36.180
7,000 British and
German soldiers.

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:37.830
That's a big army.

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:40.830
Things are beginning
to fall into place.

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:46.080
As Washington would later write,
it hit him like a ray of light.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:49.710
If I can get this army
down there in time,

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:51.270
we just might pull it off.

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:53.580
But he had to get the
French navy there.

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:56.140
He had to get de Grasse.

00:28:56.140 --> 00:29:00.210
They cross the Hudson, all
the time thinking Clinton

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:01.530
is going to attack them.

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.570
Clinton's convinced they're
going to attack them

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:05.490
via Staten Island.

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:07.680
They march toward Staten Island.

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:11.190
And then they veer to the
south and head for Philadelphia

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:13.060
as fast as they can.

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:16.350
They're in Philadelphia
when Clinton realizes, whoa,

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:20.370
they're not going after me,
they're going after Cornwallis.

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:24.360
Washington has still heard
no news from de Grasse.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:25.050
Where is he?

00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:30.510
By this time, he should
be in the Chesapeake.

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:33.780
He's made his way to
Elkton, Head of Elk,

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:38.340
they called it, the northern
tip of the Chesapeake,

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:40.200
where they're going
to begin transporting

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:44.580
a good portion of the two armies
by boats down the Chesapeake

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:46.620
towards Yorktown.

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:51.300
He still has heard
nothing from de Grasse.

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:55.140
A messenger approaches
with an express.

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:58.170
de Grasse has sailed
into the Chesapeake

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:01.620
with 28 ships of the line.

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:02.940
It's happened.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:04.330
This has happened.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:06.030
It's there.

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:09.810
Washington is so ecstatic,
he turns his horse around,

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:13.560
rides as fast as he can
to Chester, Pennsylvania--

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:16.910
if you go there now,
there's a gambling casino.

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:19.980
It's where William Penn
first stopped on his arrival

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.850
to what would
become Pennsylvania.

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:25.590
And because he knows
Rochambeau and his entourage

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:27.780
has been sailing
down the Delaware,

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:32.200
inspecting forts along the way.

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:34.620
The French are approaching
the dock at Chester.

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:40.110
And they see someone, a tall
man in regimentals, with a hat

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:43.680
in one hand, a handkerchief in
the other, jumping up and down

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:45.420
and going like this.

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.310
And they say, who could that be?

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:49.230
You know, it couldn't
be Washington.

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:52.620
He'd never do anything--
it's George Washington.

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:56.970
He is so excited that,
finally, this plan

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:59.310
that no one could have
ever really put together

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:00.910
is happening.

00:31:00.910 --> 00:31:02.910
Rochambeau disembarks.

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:03.640
They hug.

00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:06.720
And Washington was not a hugger.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:07.800
They move on.

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:09.000
It's now a race.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:12.220
We've got to get down there
as quickly as possible.

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:13.800
They make it to Mount Vernon.

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:17.760
Washington has not
been home in six years.

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.840
Four grandchildren have been
born while he's been away.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.570
Can you imagine the
emotions of that?

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:30.340
They spread out the maps on the
dining room table, strategize.

00:31:30.340 --> 00:31:30.880
They leave.

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:34.060
They're just a little ways
down the road when Washington

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:36.250
gets another message.

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:41.750
A large British naval force has
just approached the Chesapeake.

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:43.900
The French have sailed out.

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:46.000
This was four days
ago, and nothing

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:49.000
has been heard from them since.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:51.220
Now, in the history
of naval warfare,

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:54.850
if two comparable fleets
of French and British met,

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:57.400
the British always won.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:31:59.140
Now, I'm not going
to go into detail

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:00.797
about the Battle
of the Chesapeake

00:32:00.797 --> 00:32:02.630
because that's why you
have to buy the book.

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:04.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:06.820
But I will say it
has been rightfully

00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:09.700
called the most
important naval battle

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:11.830
in the history of
the world, in terms

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:15.550
of its ultimate
consequences, because

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:17.710
for the first and last time--

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:21.830
that's an exaggeration--
the French would win.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:27.580
They would force Admiral
Graves to sail back

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:31.290
to New York for repairs.

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:33.710
de Grasse returns
to the Chesapeake,

00:32:33.710 --> 00:32:37.970
just as Washington and
Rochambeau are arriving.

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:41.420
They meet on de
Grasse's flagship

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:43.640
as the armies come down.

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:47.380
And the trap closes
around Lord Cornwallis,

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:50.720
who has been assured by Clinton
in New York that, don't worry.

00:32:50.720 --> 00:32:53.720
We're going to save you.

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:57.980
Thus begins the
siege of Yorktown.

00:32:57.980 --> 00:32:59.330
The French have--

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:02.390
I think this is
Rochambeau's 17th siege.

00:33:02.390 --> 00:33:06.030
I mean, these were very
common in European warfare.

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.510
And so the French are
very good at this.

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:12.680
And it's mid-October
when Lord Cornwallis

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:17.030
is forced to surrender,
thus delivering

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:21.200
the victory that
Washington had foreseen

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:24.050
in his quest for
naval superiority.

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:27.890
Now, so many times in
working on this book

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:29.630
and studying
Washington, I wanted

00:33:29.630 --> 00:33:31.580
there to be the time
where he could go,

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:34.950
yes, I finally did it!

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:38.660
But within days of
this great victory,

00:33:38.660 --> 00:33:44.240
his stepson, Jacky Custis,
would die of camp fever.

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:46.730
This meant that as the
country was celebrating,

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:50.330
he and Martha were in
Mount Vernon in mourning.

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:53.990
They would eventually make their
way to Philadelphia and then

00:33:53.990 --> 00:33:56.000
back to the outskirts
of New York.

00:33:56.000 --> 00:34:00.350
It would take 2 and 1/2 years
to negotiate the treaty that

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:03.320
would finally end this war.

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:07.730
And it was really in
the spring of 1783

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:12.350
that Washington would meet his
greatest challenge, I think,

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:13.190
as general.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:15.650
And it would not be in warfare.

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:20.300
By this time, his army
was in Newburgh, New York.

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:23.540
The men hadn't been
paid for years.

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:26.210
his officers-- up until then,
there had been mutinies,

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:29.300
but the officers had always
been in line with Washington

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:30.350
and controlled things.

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:34.130
Now the officers were the ones
that wanted some assurance

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:37.400
that they would be compensated
for sacrificing their lives

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:39.050
for all of this time.

00:34:39.050 --> 00:34:41.630
But we barely had a government.

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:44.000
The Continental Congress
was a government

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:47.730
in name only, a window
dressing of a government.

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:50.120
They didn't have the ability
to tax the American people

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:51.679
directly.

00:34:51.679 --> 00:34:55.500
It wasn't going to happen.

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:58.940
The officers put
together a proposal

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:02.660
that they should
march on Philadelphia

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:08.360
and demand that the Continental
Congress fulfill their wishes.

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:10.700
This would have been
the military coup that

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:13.820
would have destroyed the
republican experiment

00:35:13.820 --> 00:35:15.800
of the United States.

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:22.760
Washington calls his men
together and delivers a speech

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:23.930
to his officers.

00:35:23.930 --> 00:35:27.470
They listen to it
and are unmoved.

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:29.850
By this time, Washington's
just turned 50.

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:32.750
And like many people
turning 50, he's

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:35.050
beginning to have
trouble reading.

00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:38.150
And he's just gotten his
first pair of reading glasses.

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:40.640
But no one has seen
him wearing them.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:42.090
He delivers the speech.

00:35:42.090 --> 00:35:44.107
No one's excited about it.

00:35:44.107 --> 00:35:46.190
And then he says, well, I
want to read this letter

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:48.350
from a congressional
delegate that

00:35:48.350 --> 00:35:51.290
is sympathetic to your cause.

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:52.370
He takes out the letter.

00:35:52.370 --> 00:35:54.080
And he clearly is
having trouble to read.

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:57.260
And then he says, excuse
me, I have not only

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:01.970
gone gray in the service of
my country, I am going blind.

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:05.030
He puts on those glasses
and begins to read.

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:07.940
And there is not a
dry eye in the house.

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:10.010
He leaves soon after.

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:13.760
The men unanimously agree to
do whatever George Washington

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:15.110
wants them to do.

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:18.910
Washington saved America
in that instance.

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:27.570
Finally, in November of 1783,
the British evacuate New York.

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.170
Washington has
determined that he

00:36:30.170 --> 00:36:35.060
will surrender his military
commission to Congress.

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:40.540
When George III hears that
this is Washington's intention,

00:36:40.540 --> 00:36:44.020
he says that if Washington does
that, he will be the greatest

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:46.160
man in the world.

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:47.710
And that's what Washington did.

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:49.900
He had to go to Annapolis
because Congress

00:36:49.900 --> 00:36:51.820
had moved from
Philadelphia to Annapolis

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:54.160
because they were fearful
their own army was

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:55.618
going to attack them.

00:36:55.618 --> 00:36:56.910
So they had moved to Annapolis.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:57.580
It just shows you--

00:36:57.580 --> 00:36:59.247
I mean-- and then it
took them two weeks

00:36:59.247 --> 00:37:01.510
to get a quorum
before Washington

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.580
could surrender his commission.

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:04.220
He does that.

00:37:04.220 --> 00:37:07.210
He was so overwhelmed
with emotion

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:11.020
that he had to hold his shaking
right hand with his left

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:13.420
while he delivered his speech.

00:37:13.420 --> 00:37:16.150
He then made his
way to Mount Vernon

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:20.450
and arrived at Mount Vernon
on Christmas Eve 1783.

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:23.020
Now, I finished In
the Hurricane's Eye

00:37:23.020 --> 00:37:24.250
on Christmas Eve.

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:28.150
It was a very different
kind of Christmas for me.

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:29.860
And I have to tell
you, you know,

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:33.460
I grew up thinking that
the American Revolution was

00:37:33.460 --> 00:37:38.190
a series of battles leading to
inevitable American victory,

00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:42.310
that they were a series
of stepping stones.

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:44.680
It wasn't like that at all.

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:50.320
By the fall of 1780,
everything was up in the air.

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:53.140
And as Washington
watched, it was a series

00:37:53.140 --> 00:37:58.120
of seemingly random
events, under no one's

00:37:58.120 --> 00:38:01.540
specific control, that had
to fall into place for it

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:02.360
all to work.

00:38:02.360 --> 00:38:05.530
And as he would say in a letter,
if you do not believe in God,

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:10.370
look what happened in
America in that year.

00:38:10.370 --> 00:38:14.140
And so I think the hurricane
is the appropriate metaphor

00:38:14.140 --> 00:38:16.060
for this war.

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:22.240
And it was this series of
events circling around that

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:23.770
could have gone anywhere.

00:38:23.770 --> 00:38:27.820
But always, there was
Washington, holding it together

00:38:27.820 --> 00:38:29.500
in the hurricane's eye.

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:30.896
Thank you very much.

00:38:30.896 --> 00:38:33.872
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:44.784 --> 00:38:46.610
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:48.358
We now have time
for a Q&amp;A. Please

00:38:48.358 --> 00:38:50.650
make your way to the microphone
if you have a question.

00:38:56.370 --> 00:39:00.420
Also, Mr. Philbrick will be
signing a copy of his book,

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:03.240
In the Hurricane's Eye, the
Genius of George Washington

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:06.360
and the Victory at Yorktown out
in the Searle Center directly

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:07.650
following the lecture.

00:39:17.620 --> 00:39:18.690
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:20.610
My wife says that
I'm naughty and I

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:22.090
shouldn't ask this question.

00:39:22.090 --> 00:39:29.490
But I have children in the
Naperville high school,

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:34.420
or grandchildren in
Naperville, Illinois.

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:37.970
And I went there to a
meeting some years ago

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:41.400
where Howard Zinn
was the speaker.

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:43.390
Howard has now died.

00:39:43.390 --> 00:39:49.920
And he says that Washington and
Jefferson are kin to the devil.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:51.290
And he's a Communist.

00:39:51.290 --> 00:39:54.510
And he's written the textbook
that is the most popular

00:39:54.510 --> 00:39:57.570
across the country,
teaching our kids.

00:39:57.570 --> 00:40:00.720
I'm a member of the
Chicago Presbytery.

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:04.950
This is the parent body for
all the Presbyterian churches

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:06.810
in the Chicago area.

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:10.590
And they've completed
a study on racism.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:15.340
And they start out by saying
that Washington, Jefferson, all

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:16.710
these people, were racists.

00:40:16.710 --> 00:40:18.300
They're bad people.

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:22.140
And that our big companies
were founded by racists.

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:25.980
And so our churches are
plagued with racism.

00:40:25.980 --> 00:40:28.500
And so this has
passed the Presbytery.

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:30.300
And all the churches
are now supposed

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:34.420
to be dealing with this terrible
racism because of Washington

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:35.160
and so on.

00:40:35.160 --> 00:40:35.730
Help me.

00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:37.994
What can you say to this?

00:40:37.994 --> 00:40:41.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:45.850
NATHANIEL PHILBRICK: Well,
you know, I have to--

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:49.870
I think it's important
to look at the Founding

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:53.980
Fathers in terms of the
slavery issue and all of that.

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:58.030
And I think Washington is a
very important figure in that.

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:00.610
Yes, he was a slave owner.

00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:04.990
But there was two--

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:06.820
he grew up as a slave owner.

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:10.030
He owned slaves
even before he was--

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:12.250
you know, had the
ability-- as a kid.

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:13.720
This was what he grew up in.

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.270
What is remarkable
about Washington,

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:21.970
and what I feel makes him the
only person who is deserving

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:24.730
of the title the
Father of our Country

00:41:24.730 --> 00:41:30.340
is that it was his experience
in eight years as commander

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:33.460
of the Continental
Army, he changed.

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:39.310
He recognized that it was
much more than Virginia.

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:41.740
The New Englanders he
didn't like when he first

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:47.590
arrived in Massachusetts became
his most trusted soldiers

00:41:47.590 --> 00:41:51.310
by the time it came to
the march to Yorktown.

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:54.410
Through Lafayette, who
was an abolitionist,

00:41:54.410 --> 00:42:00.370
he began to recognize
the evil of slavery.

00:42:00.370 --> 00:42:02.740
You know, there were two
people inhabiting him.

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:07.480
And yet, his life was completely
interwoven in that institution.

00:42:07.480 --> 00:42:10.180
And during the war, he wanted--

00:42:10.180 --> 00:42:15.820
as he said, he wanted to get
rid of the institution, but he--

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:18.850
many of his slaves were
owned by his wife, Martha,

00:42:18.850 --> 00:42:21.590
which made it illegal
for him to do anything.

00:42:21.590 --> 00:42:23.470
So he was embedded in all that.

00:42:23.470 --> 00:42:25.030
What's remarkable
about Washington--

00:42:25.030 --> 00:42:28.180
I think you've got to
look at people's ability

00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:34.280
to learn, and to change, and
to see things as they are.

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:35.920
And there is no
one like Washington

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:37.300
where you see that
arc, in which,

00:42:37.300 --> 00:42:39.850
finally, on his
deathbed-- you know,

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:44.320
he is dying of a throat
infection in which he

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:47.830
is basically being
strangled to death.

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:50.570
I mean, just a
horrible way to go.

00:42:50.570 --> 00:42:53.170
He had just recently
drafted a new will

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:55.870
in which he freed his slaves.

00:42:55.870 --> 00:42:58.100
And you know, there
he is on the bed.

00:42:58.100 --> 00:43:03.430
And he asks Martha to bring
the two drafts of his will.

00:43:03.430 --> 00:43:06.730
He has her destroy the old
one to keep the other one.

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:12.160
And this is his dying act, which
established a precedent that

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:14.410
was clearly seen.

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:17.630
So I think you have to
put people in the time

00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:19.660
frame in which they lived.

00:43:19.660 --> 00:43:23.170
You know, my book about
the whale ship Essex,

00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:26.380
I've had readers
who say I could not

00:43:26.380 --> 00:43:29.620
have any sympathy for a whaler
because they killed whales.

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:32.620
And my response to
that is if you grew up

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:37.360
on Nantucket in the 19th
century, you would have--

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:39.610
if you had been a boy, you
would have been a whaler.

00:43:39.610 --> 00:43:41.610
And if you had been a
woman, you would have been

00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:44.500
economically dependent on it.

00:43:44.500 --> 00:43:47.470
It's the context.

00:43:47.470 --> 00:43:48.670
You've got to see that.

00:43:48.670 --> 00:43:52.720
And if you-- it's really
not fair to the past,

00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:54.610
and it's not fair to America.

00:43:54.610 --> 00:43:57.670
I think, ultimately, you
have to look at this country

00:43:57.670 --> 00:43:59.410
as we are now.

00:43:59.410 --> 00:44:01.240
And what's great
about America is

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:04.780
we were born in a revolution,
and we will always

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:09.190
be in the midst of a revolution,
as are each generation

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:15.520
redefines the Constitution and
the Declaration of Independence

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:18.980
in terms of its own time.

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:19.840
It's messy.

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:21.470
It's disruptive.

00:44:21.470 --> 00:44:23.890
It's not terribly
efficient, but it's

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:26.560
what keeps us a free nation.

00:44:26.560 --> 00:44:30.280
And you know, we are
an aspirational people.

00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:33.950
We are always
trying to be better.

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:36.490
And that is the
essence of America.

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:39.430
And we wouldn't
have this country

00:44:39.430 --> 00:44:41.350
if it hadn't been for
George Washington.

00:44:41.350 --> 00:44:44.560
I'm now working on a
different kind of book.

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:46.990
I've spent the last two
years, my wife and I

00:44:46.990 --> 00:44:50.740
and our dog Dora have been
following Washington's travels

00:44:50.740 --> 00:44:52.240
as president.

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:55.720
When he became
president, I'm the head

00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:58.150
of 13 independent states.

00:44:58.150 --> 00:45:01.570
What do I do to create
a sense of solidarity?

00:45:01.570 --> 00:45:03.010
He went on a road trip.

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:05.800
And he didn't do it
in Air Force One.

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:07.930
He got into a carriage.

00:45:07.930 --> 00:45:14.180
He had two secretaries and six
servants, two of them enslaved.

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:16.460
And the first trip was--

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:19.070
New York was the
temporary capital.

00:45:19.070 --> 00:45:24.380
They went to 60 different
towns in New England, you know,

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:25.460
just little towns.

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:27.260
He made the decision
that he was not

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:29.150
going to stay in private
homes because that

00:45:29.150 --> 00:45:30.275
would be playing favorites.

00:45:30.275 --> 00:45:31.760
He stayed in taverns.

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:37.820
You know, taverns in 1789
were the flea-bitten Motel 6s

00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:39.830
of his day.

00:45:39.830 --> 00:45:41.660
And that's what he did.

00:45:41.660 --> 00:45:44.070
And it took him a
month to do this.

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:45.830
He went to every town.

00:45:45.830 --> 00:45:48.080
And people who were
naysayers-- and believe me,

00:45:48.080 --> 00:45:50.420
there were naysayers--

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:57.960
saw him, saw his commitment to
the concept of the government.

00:45:57.960 --> 00:46:00.620
And he created that solidarity.

00:46:00.620 --> 00:46:03.890
The longest tour would be
three months down to the South.

00:46:03.890 --> 00:46:06.290
He was a southerner,
but he had barely been

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:08.720
to North Carolina,
never south of that.

00:46:08.720 --> 00:46:12.110
He went all the way down
to Savannah, to Augusta,

00:46:12.110 --> 00:46:13.260
and back in.

00:46:13.260 --> 00:46:16.880
And so we have been
following him by car.

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:21.770
And we just cannot believe
the stamina of this guy,

00:46:21.770 --> 00:46:24.020
the commitment of this guy.

00:46:24.020 --> 00:46:27.980
You know, Washington slept here
is kind of a historical joke.

00:46:27.980 --> 00:46:29.210
It's not a joke.

00:46:29.210 --> 00:46:31.140
I mean, the amount of work--

00:46:31.140 --> 00:46:35.420
he slept in 283 different
places before the end

00:46:35.420 --> 00:46:36.680
of the Revolution.

00:46:36.680 --> 00:46:38.400
And then he did this.

00:46:38.400 --> 00:46:43.500
The guy was out there,
trying to make America work.

00:46:43.500 --> 00:46:45.620
And we would not
have the freedoms

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:48.350
we have today, we would
not have the ability

00:46:48.350 --> 00:46:51.030
to have these kinds
of conversations,

00:46:51.030 --> 00:46:53.241
if it hadn't been for
George Washington.

00:46:53.241 --> 00:46:55.646
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:01.812 --> 00:47:03.520
AUDIENCE: Well, now
I have two questions.

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:05.230
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] church.

00:47:05.230 --> 00:47:06.980
AUDIENCE: Well, now,
I have two questions.

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:10.990
You could include-- probably, in
one of those road trips of his,

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:13.570
he visited like
what, two synagogues

00:47:13.570 --> 00:47:15.208
and spoke to two
Jewish communities?

00:47:15.208 --> 00:47:16.250
NATHANIEL PHILBRICK: Yes.

00:47:16.250 --> 00:47:17.680
AUDIENCE: So you should
include that in that.

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:18.970
NATHANIEL PHILBRICK:
Oh, yeah, yeah, no.

00:47:18.970 --> 00:47:19.780
It's great.

00:47:19.780 --> 00:47:22.750
OK, so he did the
New England tour.

00:47:22.750 --> 00:47:25.340
But Rhode Island-- and I went
to college in Rhode Island.

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:28.420
And Rhode Islanders
are Rhode Islanders,

00:47:28.420 --> 00:47:30.880
with Roger Williams,
the independent man.

00:47:30.880 --> 00:47:33.190
And they hadn't ratified
the Constitution

00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:36.430
by the time Washington went
on his New England tour.

00:47:36.430 --> 00:47:39.520
So he very carefully
took-- there are three ways

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:42.070
to get north from New Haven.

00:47:42.070 --> 00:47:45.340
You could take where I-95 goes
along the coast, which would

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:46.960
go through to Rhode Island.

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:48.430
Or you could take--

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:51.790
go the inland route to
Hartford, Springfield,

00:47:51.790 --> 00:47:52.900
and then to Boston.

00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:55.870
He went inland and never
went to Rhode Island.

00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:57.740
That put a lot of
pressure on the state,

00:47:57.740 --> 00:48:00.040
and that was absolutely
his intention.

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:04.360
And so within a few months time,
they ratify the Constitution.

00:48:04.360 --> 00:48:06.370
That summer, in 1790--

00:48:06.370 --> 00:48:07.930
and this is when
all sorts of stuff

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:09.700
is happening in his government.

00:48:09.700 --> 00:48:14.710
Jefferson has just become
his secretary of state.

00:48:14.710 --> 00:48:16.180
He and Hamilton are--

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:18.670
their uneasy relationship
is beginning.

00:48:18.670 --> 00:48:23.460
They're trying to work out the
compromises that will create

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:26.080
a tax structure and all that.

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:31.500
And Washington decides it's
time to visit Rhode Island.

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:36.280
So he and Thomas Jefferson get
on one of John Brown's packets

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:37.960
and sail to Newport.

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:42.130
And there, he got a
letter from a member

00:48:42.130 --> 00:48:48.640
of the Jewish congregation in
Newport, one of the oldest--

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:51.410
there's a little argument who's
the oldest, whether New York

00:48:51.410 --> 00:48:52.300
or Newport.

00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:54.190
But this one was really old.

00:48:54.190 --> 00:48:57.460
And Washington's
response, which Jefferson

00:48:57.460 --> 00:49:01.000
may have had a hand in it
because religious freedom

00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:03.880
was a big part of where
Jefferson came from,

00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:08.320
in which it is still
looked to today as one

00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:11.050
of the most eloquent
expressions of the importance

00:49:11.050 --> 00:49:13.310
of religious freedom
ever written.

00:49:13.310 --> 00:49:16.030
And it was during this trip.

00:49:16.030 --> 00:49:21.610
And so my wife and I sailed
to Newport to do this

00:49:21.610 --> 00:49:25.930
and had just the most fantastic
tour of the synagogue.

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:28.615
And this is a living history.

00:49:31.270 --> 00:49:33.880
It's not a statue
or a museum exhibit

00:49:33.880 --> 00:49:36.160
with dust gathering around it.

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:38.530
What Washington--
by going there,

00:49:38.530 --> 00:49:42.700
Washington created a ripple
effect which these communities

00:49:42.700 --> 00:49:43.950
are still feeling.

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:48.490
And in a way, all
history is local.

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:50.740
You know, Tip O'Neill used
to say that about politics.

00:49:53.360 --> 00:49:55.960
And that's what's been really
fun following Washington

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:59.920
on this is I've contacted
archivists, librarians

00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:02.260
in each one-- all
of these towns,

00:50:02.260 --> 00:50:06.280
and looking for records
of the citizens' responses

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:07.600
to Washington.

00:50:07.600 --> 00:50:10.150
And just about every
one of these towns

00:50:10.150 --> 00:50:11.620
has a plaque somewhere.

00:50:11.620 --> 00:50:14.710
And then somewhere in the middle
of the 19th century, someone's

00:50:14.710 --> 00:50:19.270
great-grandmother,
who was about 90,

00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:23.239
had a memory of when
Washington came to town.

00:50:23.239 --> 00:50:25.554
AUDIENCE: So my
second question was,

00:50:25.554 --> 00:50:30.760
how is Admiral de Grasse
remembered in French history?

00:50:30.760 --> 00:50:34.480
NATHANIEL PHILBRICK: Yeah, well,
Admiral de Grasse, great hero

00:50:34.480 --> 00:50:36.220
from our perspective.

00:50:36.220 --> 00:50:38.860
Unfortunately-- and
Washington and Rochambeau

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:41.290
both complained
about it. de Grasse

00:50:41.290 --> 00:50:44.050
was a good admiral
but very impulsive,

00:50:44.050 --> 00:50:47.890
and did not always
have the best judgment.

00:50:47.890 --> 00:50:49.330
And he was lucky.

00:50:49.330 --> 00:50:53.170
He went up against a very
mediocre British admiral.

00:50:53.170 --> 00:50:57.220
It was nobody's first choice to
be meeting de Grasse in this.

00:50:57.220 --> 00:50:59.350
A few months later,
de Grasse goes up

00:50:59.350 --> 00:51:03.930
against one of the Britain's
great admirals, Admiral Rodney,

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:05.740
in the West Indies.

00:51:05.740 --> 00:51:08.530
It's known as the
Battle of the Saints.

00:51:08.530 --> 00:51:17.730
And de Grasse's fleet would get
completely routed by Rodney.

00:51:17.730 --> 00:51:20.690
de Grasse had the
biggest flagship

00:51:20.690 --> 00:51:22.400
in all of the navies anywhere.

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:27.935
He would be forced to surrender
his sword to Rodney, which

00:51:27.935 --> 00:51:29.450
is just absolutely humiliating.

00:51:29.450 --> 00:51:33.140
He would be a prisoner in
London for a number of months.

00:51:33.140 --> 00:51:38.210
And it was a huge
embarrassment to France.

00:51:38.210 --> 00:51:41.450
And so he is not
remembered fondly.

00:51:41.450 --> 00:51:45.110
But the one thing that was good
about that from my standpoint

00:51:45.110 --> 00:51:48.380
as a historian was that there
were court martial proceedings

00:51:48.380 --> 00:51:50.300
following that great defeat.

00:51:50.300 --> 00:51:52.580
And as a consequence,
the logbooks

00:51:52.580 --> 00:51:55.280
of many of the
captains that were

00:51:55.280 --> 00:51:58.550
at the Battle of the Chesapeake
were preserved and are still

00:51:58.550 --> 00:52:01.610
at the archives in Paris.

00:52:01.610 --> 00:52:05.120
And they were an absolute
treasure trove for me

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:07.990
when it came to recreating
the Battle of the Chesapeake.

00:52:07.990 --> 00:52:11.160
Because you know, it's--

00:52:11.160 --> 00:52:14.510
past accounts lean very
heavily on English accounts,

00:52:14.510 --> 00:52:16.850
I think largely because
they're in English.

00:52:16.850 --> 00:52:21.770
The fact is, the French
won and their accounts--

00:52:21.770 --> 00:52:24.930
and boy, there's a lot of them.

00:52:24.930 --> 00:52:26.230
And so I found the--

00:52:26.230 --> 00:52:27.090
And I'm a sailor.

00:52:27.090 --> 00:52:30.730
I grew up sailing, racing those
great magnificent yachts known

00:52:30.730 --> 00:52:31.230
as Sunfish.

00:52:31.230 --> 00:52:33.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:33.940 --> 00:52:36.080
I am a Sunfish North
American champion.

00:52:36.080 --> 00:52:37.730
That's no laughing matter.

00:52:37.730 --> 00:52:39.410
[WEAK APPLAUSE]

00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:40.430
[LAUGHS]

00:52:40.430 --> 00:52:45.170
But as a sailor, it
was so exciting to read

00:52:45.170 --> 00:52:46.430
these logbooks.

00:52:46.430 --> 00:52:49.340
And I worked with a
translator in Quebec,

00:52:49.340 --> 00:52:51.110
who helped me with this--

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:54.560
to read these accounts
of this battle.

00:52:54.560 --> 00:52:57.740
It's just a
fascinating slugfest.

00:52:57.740 --> 00:53:01.820
And for me, as a historian,
that's the juice.

00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:08.390
You're getting those sources
that haven't been used,

00:53:08.390 --> 00:53:11.780
those primary sources that
speak to you as if they

00:53:11.780 --> 00:53:14.102
were speaking to you today.

00:53:14.102 --> 00:53:15.935
SPEAKER: We have time
for one more question.

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:20.030
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:53:20.030 --> 00:53:25.460
Several years ago, Dr. D.
James Kennedy in Florida

00:53:25.460 --> 00:53:27.600
spoke about George Washington.

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:30.140
And he said that in
modern times it's

00:53:30.140 --> 00:53:34.760
been popular to
denigrate or downplay

00:53:34.760 --> 00:53:38.570
the importance of his
Christian faith in his life

00:53:38.570 --> 00:53:41.480
and in his decisions.

00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:44.500
And he went through
quite a teaching

00:53:44.500 --> 00:53:48.440
on showing the importance
of his Christian faith.

00:53:48.440 --> 00:53:49.910
Could you speak to that, please?

00:53:49.910 --> 00:53:51.470
NATHANIEL PHILBRICK: Yeah, yeah.

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:56.090
Washington's faith is
a fascinating thing.

00:53:56.090 --> 00:53:59.710
You know, I think--

00:53:59.710 --> 00:54:04.500
and it really ties into his
role as a battlefield commander,

00:54:04.500 --> 00:54:05.000
too.

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:08.090
When he experienced the--

00:54:08.090 --> 00:54:10.970
early on, when he was
representing Britain

00:54:10.970 --> 00:54:15.860
in the French and Indian War, he
was there at Braddock's defeat,

00:54:15.860 --> 00:54:20.660
where every officer on
horseback was killed or wounded

00:54:20.660 --> 00:54:21.890
except for him.

00:54:21.890 --> 00:54:25.850
And he had three bullet holes
through his uniform, one

00:54:25.850 --> 00:54:27.350
through his hat.

00:54:27.350 --> 00:54:32.140
And his best friend
and doctor, Dr. Craik,

00:54:32.140 --> 00:54:34.760
who would actually be
there by his bedside

00:54:34.760 --> 00:54:42.620
when he died, talked to
several Indian warriors that

00:54:42.620 --> 00:54:46.220
were there and told him
how everyone was aiming

00:54:46.220 --> 00:54:48.830
at George Washington and
couldn't believe they

00:54:48.830 --> 00:54:49.610
didn't kill him.

00:54:49.610 --> 00:54:53.030
And he was-- they gave him a
name of the person who cannot

00:54:53.030 --> 00:54:55.660
be killed, or words
to that effect.

00:54:55.660 --> 00:54:59.330
And I think he-- you know, that
gave him a profoundly spiritual

00:54:59.330 --> 00:55:02.550
sense of mission.

00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:04.610
You can see it in his
letters after that.

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:10.920
He worshipped in the
Church of England,

00:55:10.920 --> 00:55:13.910
which became complicated
after the Revolution

00:55:13.910 --> 00:55:17.480
because you then had the
founding of Episcopalian faith.

00:55:17.480 --> 00:55:19.305
And that was a rough transition.

00:55:19.305 --> 00:55:20.180
And it's interesting.

00:55:20.180 --> 00:55:28.190
After the Revolution, he
would cease taking communion.

00:55:28.190 --> 00:55:30.245
He would attend
church every Sunday.

00:55:32.910 --> 00:55:36.800
But no one knows
quite what was--

00:55:36.800 --> 00:55:40.040
had presiding over that
many men killed, you know,

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:44.210
what was he thinking about
slave-- who knows what it was?

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:46.550
Or maybe he was
just angry at what

00:55:46.550 --> 00:55:49.100
was happening in terms of
the new Episcopalian church.

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:50.420
Who knows?

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:54.020
But spirituality was
absolutely important to him.

00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:05.720
His letters, his whole
demeanor was highly spiritual.

00:56:05.720 --> 00:56:08.030
But I think, also,
ultimately, with Washington,

00:56:08.030 --> 00:56:13.760
who was an intensely private
man for someone so public,

00:56:13.760 --> 00:56:16.890
it was ultimately his own thing.

00:56:16.890 --> 00:56:20.060
And from my mind, I think that
I have all the more respect

00:56:20.060 --> 00:56:21.710
for him because of that.

00:56:21.710 --> 00:56:23.828
Well, thank you very much.

00:56:23.828 --> 00:56:24.620
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:56:24.620 --> 00:56:27.970
[APPLAUSE]

